{"_meta":{"description":"Normalized residential base load shape for the PG&E residential class (load profile E1/RES), calendar 2024, by month (0=Jan..11=Dec) x day type [0=weekday Mon-Fri, 1=weekend Sat-Sun] x hour (0..23, hour-beginning, fixed UTC-8 / Pacific Standard Time, aligned with data/simulate/caiso_2024_hourly.csv). Holidays are treated as weekdays (ignored).","kind":"observed","source":"PG&E Dynamic Load Profiles, 'Residential' (E1) daily hourly profile file e1_dynamic_2001.xls (tab-separated text, Profile/Date + 24 hour-ending columns, data 01/01/2000 to 10/13/2026), linked from PG&E 'Understanding Energy Use and Prices'","url":"https://www.pge.com/assets/rates/csv/e1_dynamic_2001.xls","page_url":"https://www.pge.com/assets/rates/tariffs/energy_use_prices.shtml","help_url":"https://www.pge.com/tariffs/load_profiles_help.html","note":"PG&E help page, quoted: 'The numbers in the templates are hourly average demands measured in kW for a sample of customers.' and 'Posting hourly demands mean that the numbers in the templates are both kW and kWh.' Energy-use page, quoted: 'Dynamic Load Profiles: Choose a profile and download average load shapes in a spreadsheet. The data begins January 1, 2000, and is updated several times each week' and 'PG&E will post the same profile with two different labels, E1 and RES'. PG&E's 2024 Static Load Profiles page says only Agricultural and Street Lighting have static profiles ('All other classes must use Dynamic load profiles'), so the residential DYNAMIC class profile is used. Processing: the file is in local prevailing time (DST; e.g. 03/10/2024 hour-ending 3:00 am is 0), so each value was placed at its local hour-beginning, converted to fixed UTC-8 (PDT hours shift 1 h earlier), and then averaged over all 2024 PST days of each month and day type (nonexistent spring-forward hour dropped; the fall-back extra hour is not separately reported by PG&E, so PST 01:00 on 2024-11-03 is missing and that hour is averaged from the remaining days). UNCONFIRMED: whether the E1/RES sample load is gross consumption or net of customer rooftop solar; its midday summer dip suggests it may include NEM customers' net load. This is the average customer of the whole class (all climate zones, all end uses), not a specific home.","normalization":"avg_kw = mean hourly kW (=kWh in that hour) of the average E1 customer. shape = avg_kw / sum(avg_kw over 24 h) so each (month, daytype) 24-vector sums to 1 (fraction of that day's energy). day_kwh = sum of avg_kw over 24 h (average daily kWh for that month/daytype). To build a home's base load for a month: hourly = shape[m][d][h] * day_kwh[m][d] * k, with k chosen so the month's 2024 total equals the target base kWh.","retrieved":"2026-10-24"},"profile":"E1/RES","year":2024,"time_basis":"hour-beginning, fixed UTC-8 (PST, no DST)","daytypes":["weekday","weekend"],"days_counted":[[23,8],[21,8],[21,10],[22,8],[23,8],[20,10],[23,8],[22,9],[21,9],[23,8],[21,9],[22,9]],"avg_kw":[[[0.6598,0.61372,0.57464,0.55328,0.55403,0.58737,0.66022,0.73289,0.71264,0.67309,0.63983,0.61673,0.60541,0.59367,0.58971,0.62032,0.70756,0.85223,0.93539,0.94164,0.922,0.88593,0.8059,0.71183],[0.68986,0.64031,0.59613,0.56888,0.55858,0.57034,0.60692,0.66918,0.71122,0.72519,0.71812,0.71119,0.71268,0.71193,0.71313,0.73061,0.7938,0.89843,0.94292,0.93501,0.91004,0.87338,0.80201,0.71483]],[[0.63089,0.58785,0.55325,0.53293,0.53608,0.57181,0.65068,0.70586,0.66134,0.61417,0.58229,0.56495,0.55564,0.54473,0.53794,0.55502,0.61248,0.74068,0.86084,0.88828,0.87298,0.84264,0.77061,0.68478],[0.66328,0.62001,0.57882,0.55285,0.54453,0.55752,0.59772,0.65076,0.67954,0.68335,0.67457,0.66159,0.6505,0.63918,0.63451,0.64586,0.68861,0.78376,0.86021,0.86556,0.84838,0.819,0.75665,0.67672]],[[0.56289,0.52589,0.50003,0.4931,0.51308,0.57196,0.64153,0.62478,0.57538,0.53935,0.51849,0.50555,0.49685,0.48881,0.49047,0.5133,0.56908,0.66528,0.7658,0.80232,0.78864,0.73693,0.66026,0.60738],[0.58948,0.54828,0.49546,0.50296,0.50563,0.53188,0